The current implementation is lifecycle-only: command/tool hooks take no payload and return only an integer status; manifests lack publisher/signature/schema metadata; and the host links no explicit capability calls. I’ll replace that gap with three bounded layers: a versioned WIT contract and guest bindings, typed Rust invocation/manifest models with validation, and a sandboxed executable ABI module that moves real request/response bytes and exposes only declared host capabilities. Legacy v1 packages will remain readable so unrelated migration coverage is not broken.
  → Define v2 WIT contract, typed SDK models, validation, and guest bindings while preserving v1 compatibility
  • Implement bounded executable plugin ABI host with typed payload transport and explicit capability calls
  • Add a real reference WebAssembly component/module conformance journey for payloads, streaming, cancellation, and denial
  • Run formatting, focused tests, Codify review/guard, exact verify command, spec completion, trace, commit/push, and handoff
