## User preferences

- when the assistant framed Keith as “multi-agent handoff,” the user corrected: “Its identical to chatting planing and collaborating with humans” and “what people really need is their Jarvis” -> center future product reasoning on one pervasive operational intelligence, not an agent dashboard or bot org chart. [Task 1]
- when the user said “look up Jarvis … [for] the central point” -> use JARVIS as the reference for continuous presence, leverage, anticipation, and direct operation. [Task 1]

## Reusable knowledge

- Keith should be one persistent identity and world model spanning communications, projects/company, relationships, devices/environments, research, schedules, tools, and infrastructure. Rooms, DMs, voice, desktop, mobile, and channels are projections of the same Keith state; internal specialists should normally remain hidden faculties. [Task 1]
- Product north star: “Keith turns one person into a fully augmented organization.” Conversation is the interface, not the product itself: core value is continuous situational awareness, exception surfacing, anticipation, direct tool operation, coordination, durable commitments, and permissioned, observable, reversible execution. The later teammate rollout again distinguished this persistent operational intelligence from a chatbot or roster of temporary digital coworkers. [Task 1][Task 2]

## Failures and how to do differently

- Symptom: product work leads with “multi-agent chat,” shallow personalities, or copied Slack/Discord surfaces. Cause: the framing misses the JARVIS central point. Fix: begin from Keith’s unified state, operational presence, and leverage; use surfaces as projections, not the architecture. [Task 1]

